1
00:00:15,272 --> 00:00:16,273
Come, Sherlock.

2
00:00:16,315 --> 00:00:17,483
You should eat.

3
00:00:17,524 --> 00:00:21,946
Oh, I'm fine. I ate
too much last night.

4
00:00:21,987 --> 00:00:23,614
You've put on some weight.

5
00:00:23,656 --> 00:00:25,365
'Bout half a stone, I'd say.

6
00:00:25,407 --> 00:00:26,617
[gasps]

7
00:00:26,659 --> 00:00:27,827
[WATSON]
He noticed that?

8
00:00:27,868 --> 00:00:29,495
[knocking]

9
00:00:29,536 --> 00:00:31,079
- -[door squeaks]
- -Huh?

10
00:00:31,121 --> 00:00:33,791
Sherlock. You've a visitor.

11
00:00:33,833 --> 00:00:37,336
[WATSON grunts]
I'll come down.

12
00:00:39,296 --> 00:00:43,843
Yes? Can I help you?

13
00:00:43,884 --> 00:00:45,594
Good morning to you.

14
00:00:45,636 --> 00:00:48,096
Might Mr. Holmes be in today?

15
00:00:48,138 --> 00:00:50,599
[WATSON]
Indeed. You must be a client.

16
00:00:50,641 --> 00:00:52,142
Come in.

17
00:00:54,353 --> 00:00:58,023
[MYCROFT] Not to be rude,
but you shouldn't overindulge.

18
00:00:58,064 --> 00:01:00,734
You seem to have gained
about half a stone of late.

19
00:01:00,776 --> 00:01:04,363
What?! But just how
would you know that?

20
00:01:04,404 --> 00:01:06,239
[MYCROFT] I noticed the
buttonholes on your vest

21
00:01:06,281 --> 00:01:07,825
have been enlarged.

22
00:01:07,867 --> 00:01:09,869
The tailoring appears
to be rather hasty,

23
00:01:09,910 --> 00:01:13,079
so seems it was
a recent change.

24
00:01:13,121 --> 00:01:15,749
Factoring in your
waist size and height.

25
00:01:15,791 --> 00:01:19,378
It's quite clear to see that
you have gained half a stone

26
00:01:19,419 --> 00:01:22,631
[grunts] Who the devil are you?

27
00:02:54,598 --> 00:02:55,808
[MYCROFT grunts]

28
00:02:57,350 --> 00:02:59,352
[HOLMES grunting]

29
00:02:59,394 --> 00:03:00,395
- -[HOLMES grunts]
- -[MYCROFT chuckles]

30
00:03:00,437 --> 00:03:01,271
[grunts]

31
00:03:01,313 --> 00:03:02,982
[HOLMES grunts, gasps]

32
00:03:10,572 --> 00:03:12,783
[WATSON gasps]
Sherlock!

33
00:03:12,825 --> 00:03:13,867
[HOLMES groans]

34
00:03:13,909 --> 00:03:14,868
Huh?

35
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:17,496
[MYCROFT chuckles]

36
00:03:17,537 --> 00:03:23,002
[MYCROFT] Today's test
makes it 673 to nothing, Sherly.

37
00:03:23,043 --> 00:03:24,377
I'm left to wonder...

38
00:03:24,419 --> 00:03:27,714
How much longer before
you make me taste defeat?

39
00:03:27,756 --> 00:03:28,507
[WATSON grunts]

40
00:03:28,548 --> 00:03:30,134
Soon enough, Brother.

41
00:03:30,175 --> 00:03:32,136
[WATSON gasps]
This man is your brother?

42
00:03:32,177 --> 00:03:35,347
Lamentably so.
My elder brother, Mycroft.

43
00:03:35,388 --> 00:03:37,557
Delightful, isn't it?

44
00:03:37,599 --> 00:03:40,811
A new revolver developed
by my unit that shoots paint.

45
00:03:40,853 --> 00:03:43,313
[HOLMES]
Who cares? Get out!

46
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:49,319
[WATSON]
Pardon me.

47
00:03:49,361 --> 00:03:52,739
Are you truly
Sherlock's brother?

48
00:03:52,781 --> 00:03:54,324
[HOLMES]
Tch.

49
00:03:54,366 --> 00:03:55,701
Indeed.

50
00:03:55,742 --> 00:03:58,745
My brother and I have taken
very different paths in life.

51
00:03:58,787 --> 00:04:00,497
What do you want?!

52
00:04:00,539 --> 00:04:03,876
Did you come here solely
to make snide remarks?

53
00:04:04,626 --> 00:04:09,381
I'm actually here to give
you a warning, Sherlock.

54
00:04:09,422 --> 00:04:12,676
[WATSON]

55
00:04:12,718 --> 00:04:14,678
Beware of women, dear brother.

56
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:16,262
Wha--?

57
00:04:16,304 --> 00:04:19,058
Seeing through the
deceits of the fairer sex

58
00:04:19,099 --> 00:04:22,602
is even more difficult than
tracing the threads of a crime.

59
00:04:23,937 --> 00:04:26,272
Well, I shall be
taking my leave now.

60
00:04:26,314 --> 00:04:29,026
Reflect upon my advice, Sherly.

61
00:04:30,194 --> 00:04:31,319
[HUDSON giggles]

62
00:04:31,361 --> 00:04:32,571
[WATSON grunts]

63
00:04:36,033 --> 00:04:39,786
You're looking rather pale.
Are you feeling poorly?

64
00:04:39,828 --> 00:04:43,665
[HOLMES]
Mycroft has that effect on me.

65
00:04:43,707 --> 00:04:46,210
[WATSON]
That carriage he's traveling in.

66
00:04:46,251 --> 00:04:48,837
Is he employed in
some government office?

67
00:04:48,879 --> 00:04:52,632
Some office?
It's not nearly so simple.

68
00:04:56,261 --> 00:04:59,598
No. He is the government.
In the flesh.

69
00:04:59,639 --> 00:05:01,600
He sounds important.

70
00:05:01,641 --> 00:05:05,229
[HOLMES groans] Let's stop
talking about my brother, 'kay?

71
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:07,773
Instead, look at this.

72
00:05:07,814 --> 00:05:09,191
Read it.

73
00:05:10,692 --> 00:05:14,154
It appears undated,
without signature or address.

74
00:05:15,572 --> 00:05:17,074
[WATSON] "There will
call upon you tonight,

75
00:05:17,116 --> 00:05:18,450
at a quarter to eight o'clock,

76
00:05:18,491 --> 00:05:20,410
a gentleman who
desires to consult you

77
00:05:20,452 --> 00:05:23,413
upon a matter of the
very deepest moment.

78
00:05:23,455 --> 00:05:25,540
Be in your chamber
then at that hour,

79
00:05:25,582 --> 00:05:29,044
and do not take it amiss
if your visitor wear a mask."

80
00:05:29,086 --> 00:05:31,088
[HOLMES]
Your thoughts, John?

81
00:05:31,130 --> 00:05:33,465
First, this handwriting
is that of a man.

82
00:05:33,506 --> 00:05:36,969
Further, I believe him to be a
well-educated man of means.

83
00:05:37,010 --> 00:05:38,595
This particularly fine paper

84
00:05:38,637 --> 00:05:41,640
could not be had for less
than half a crown, I'd say.

85
00:05:41,681 --> 00:05:44,101
See? You can do it if you try.

86
00:05:44,143 --> 00:05:47,312
Now then, about the paper.

87
00:05:47,353 --> 00:05:51,399
Hold it up to the light.

88
00:05:51,441 --> 00:05:54,569
[WATSON] Are these the
initials of the paper mill?

89
00:05:54,611 --> 00:05:58,448
[HOLMES] The "PGt" is
German for Papiergesellschaft.

90
00:05:58,490 --> 00:06:01,034
It means paper company.

91
00:06:01,076 --> 00:06:04,621
"Eg" most likely
stands for Egria,

92
00:06:04,663 --> 00:06:07,415
A city in Bohemia's
German region.

93
00:06:09,918 --> 00:06:12,963
[WATSON] So the man
who sent you this letter

94
00:06:13,005 --> 00:06:16,925
is a masked German
from the Kingdom of Bohemia.

95
00:06:16,967 --> 00:06:18,218
Is that right?

96
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:20,637
[knocking]

97
00:06:20,679 --> 00:06:21,930
[MYCROFT]
Yes, come in.

98
00:06:21,972 --> 00:06:23,890
[door opens]

99
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:30,981
[MYCROFT] Forgive me
calling for you this late.

100
00:06:33,108 --> 00:06:35,443
[MYCROFT]
Lieutenant Colonel Moriarty.

101
00:06:35,485 --> 00:06:38,322
Sir, what does the director
of the intelligence service

102
00:06:38,363 --> 00:06:41,533
need with me, an officer
of the Indian Army?

103
00:06:41,574 --> 00:06:43,368
[MYCROFT]
I understand that you, Colonel,

104
00:06:43,409 --> 00:06:44,744
with limited manpower,

105
00:06:44,786 --> 00:06:46,788
secretly wiped out
the distribution routes

106
00:06:46,830 --> 00:06:50,250
of that new kind of opium
making its way around London.

107
00:06:50,292 --> 00:06:54,171
[ALBERT] Hm. An internal
investigation, sir?

108
00:06:54,213 --> 00:06:56,923
[MYCROFT]
Yes. And as a result,

109
00:06:56,965 --> 00:07:00,344
I found your qualifications
to be quite sufficient.

110
00:07:05,015 --> 00:07:08,143
[MYCROFT] I would appreciate
your assistance in this matter.

111
00:07:11,021 --> 00:07:15,525
[MYCROFT] Irene Adler.
An American,born in New Jersey.

112
00:07:15,567 --> 00:07:18,111
Retired following a
career as the prima donna

113
00:07:18,153 --> 00:07:20,989
of the Imperial Opera of Warsaw.

114
00:07:21,031 --> 00:07:23,992
Publicly, she is well
known as a former actress.

115
00:07:24,034 --> 00:07:26,036
But secretly she's a courtesan,

116
00:07:26,078 --> 00:07:29,248
with a multinational
clientele of nobility.

117
00:07:29,289 --> 00:07:31,041
I'm tasking you
with the retrieval

118
00:07:31,083 --> 00:07:34,669
of certain classified
papers stolen by this woman.

119
00:07:34,711 --> 00:07:36,504
These classified papers--

120
00:07:36,546 --> 00:07:43,970
they are more than just
correspondence, I gather.

121
00:07:44,012 --> 00:07:47,099
What they contain could
be far more devastating

122
00:07:47,140 --> 00:07:49,059
than you can imagine,
I assure you.

123
00:07:49,101 --> 00:07:53,939
It concerns the Crown, and the
stability of the Empire itself.

124
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:56,149
[ALBERT]
Whoever retrieves these papers

125
00:07:56,191 --> 00:07:59,194
will unavoidably learn
the contents within.

126
00:07:59,236 --> 00:08:02,864
If he's making such a determined
effort not to reveal them,

127
00:08:02,906 --> 00:08:05,742
this must be quite
the serious affair.

128
00:08:06,952 --> 00:08:10,538
Understood, sir.
I will see to it.

129
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:12,958
We're counting on you, Colonel.

130
00:08:12,999 --> 00:08:15,043
Your orders are as follows.

131
00:08:15,085 --> 00:08:21,091
Retrieve the stolen documents,
and liquidate Irene Adler.

132
00:08:27,097 --> 00:08:29,515
Classified documents?
Intriguing.

133
00:08:29,557 --> 00:08:34,479
Indeed. Apparently, serious
enough to rock the whole nation.

134
00:08:34,520 --> 00:08:36,731
Is there anything else
that we should know?

135
00:08:36,773 --> 00:08:39,734
Any demands made
by this Adler woman?

136
00:08:39,776 --> 00:08:41,111
No. Up to now,

137
00:08:41,153 --> 00:08:44,364
there hasn't been a single
demand issued for their return.

138
00:08:44,406 --> 00:08:48,410
In fact, she's stayed hidden,
without making any contact.

139
00:08:48,452 --> 00:08:50,662
[LOUIS]
She stole something so critical,

140
00:08:50,703 --> 00:08:53,290
yet has made no demands.

141
00:08:53,332 --> 00:08:57,336
It's rather curious,
don't you agree?

142
00:09:12,184 --> 00:09:15,437
[WILHELM] I apologize,
and hope you will forgive me

143
00:09:15,479 --> 00:09:17,689
for arriving in this mask.

144
00:09:17,730 --> 00:09:19,816
I am the King of Bohemia.

145
00:09:19,858 --> 00:09:24,446
Wilhelm Gottsreich Sigismond von
Ormstein, and I seek your help.

146
00:09:24,488 --> 00:09:25,613
You're a king?

147
00:09:25,655 --> 00:09:28,492
Well, that was obvious
the moment you walked in.

148
00:09:28,533 --> 00:09:32,245
Sorry. Under these
circumstances,

149
00:09:32,287 --> 00:09:35,790
I could not very well
send another in my place.

150
00:09:35,832 --> 00:09:39,711
I see, go on.

151
00:09:39,752 --> 00:09:42,464
The whole affair
began five years ago.

152
00:09:42,506 --> 00:09:45,008
It was when I was
staying in Warsaw.

153
00:09:45,050 --> 00:09:47,802
I fell in love with
a beautiful woman.

154
00:09:47,844 --> 00:09:51,848
This woman's name
is Irene Adler.

155
00:09:51,890 --> 00:09:53,808
Yes. It's all
becoming clear now.

156
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:55,935
Sherlock, do you
know this woman?

157
00:09:55,977 --> 00:09:57,687
I've never actually met her.

158
00:09:57,729 --> 00:10:00,148
But she's famous
in the underworld.

159
00:10:00,190 --> 00:10:04,694
So then, what does
this woman have over you?

160
00:10:04,736 --> 00:10:08,365
She has a photograph.
If anyone were to see,

161
00:10:08,407 --> 00:10:10,742
they might naturally
assume we would be wed.

162
00:10:10,783 --> 00:10:12,285
[HOLMES chuckles]
Oh, you don't say?

163
00:10:12,327 --> 00:10:14,371
I admire this woman's pluck!

164
00:10:14,413 --> 00:10:17,082
So, she's making you pay
for dumping her, is that it?

165
00:10:17,123 --> 00:10:20,835
No! Sherlock, don't do that!
Stop being so boorish!

166
00:10:20,877 --> 00:10:23,922
It just so happens
that I am committed to marry

167
00:10:23,963 --> 00:10:25,798
another woman in
the very near future.

168
00:10:25,840 --> 00:10:27,467
[WATSON gasps]

169
00:10:27,509 --> 00:10:29,344
[WILHELM]
But Adler

170
00:10:29,386 --> 00:10:31,179
has threatened to
send my dear betrothed

171
00:10:31,221 --> 00:10:33,306
the photograph
before our nuptials!

172
00:10:33,348 --> 00:10:36,518
Brilliant! Miss Adler
sounds riveting!

173
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:37,936
[BOTH]
Uh?

174
00:10:37,977 --> 00:10:40,021
[WILHELM]
Three hundred pounds in gold.

175
00:10:40,063 --> 00:10:42,315
And a note for
another seven hundred.

176
00:10:42,357 --> 00:10:45,985
[WATSON gasps] Why-- Why
that's a thousand pounds!

177
00:10:46,027 --> 00:10:49,030
That would pay our rent for
the next decade, and then some.

178
00:10:49,072 --> 00:10:51,157
[WILHELM]
Here is the woman's address.

179
00:10:51,199 --> 00:10:54,953
I ask you to please
retrieve the photograph.

180
00:10:59,791 --> 00:11:02,794
[WILHELM]
I am glad to have met you.

181
00:11:02,835 --> 00:11:06,423
Good night,
Mister Sherlock Holmes.

182
00:11:33,617 --> 00:11:39,664
[ADLER chuckles] Perhaps I made
that lie a little too elaborate.

183
00:11:40,624 --> 00:11:46,087
And yet... it needed
to be realistic.

184
00:11:47,255 --> 00:11:49,048
[ADLER]
How else would I lead on

185
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:52,218
the greatest
detective in London?

186
00:11:53,470 --> 00:11:56,389
I hope you'll hurry
and see me soon,

187
00:11:56,431 --> 00:11:59,351
Master Detective Holmes.

188
00:12:09,027 --> 00:12:11,655
[HOLMES]
I apologize for calling so late.

189
00:12:11,696 --> 00:12:15,241
Ahem. Allow me to introduce
myself, Sherlock Holmes,

190
00:12:15,283 --> 00:12:17,577
the consulting detective.

191
00:12:17,619 --> 00:12:19,663
You had some plumbing
work done here recently

192
00:12:19,704 --> 00:12:21,206
if I'm not mistaken.

193
00:12:21,247 --> 00:12:24,167
It seems at some
point during that work,

194
00:12:24,209 --> 00:12:27,170
a criminal wanted for murder
slipped in with the laborers,

195
00:12:27,212 --> 00:12:30,256
and concealed a piece of
evidence within your home.

196
00:12:30,298 --> 00:12:33,468
I ask for your assistance
with the search.

197
00:12:36,388 --> 00:12:37,389
What's this?

198
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:39,098
A smoke bomb, Watson.

199
00:12:39,140 --> 00:12:41,934
We're going to use it to make
her think the house is on fire.

200
00:12:41,976 --> 00:12:43,311
We're doing what?!

201
00:12:43,353 --> 00:12:45,188
See here, John.

202
00:12:45,230 --> 00:12:48,692
When an unforeseen incident,
such as a house fire occurs,

203
00:12:48,733 --> 00:12:50,443
people instinctively
become concerned

204
00:12:50,485 --> 00:12:52,737
about what they find important.

205
00:12:52,778 --> 00:12:56,241
Don't worry; I've had great
success with this trick before.

206
00:12:56,282 --> 00:13:00,036
[WATSON] Well, everything
depends on how Adler responds.

207
00:13:00,078 --> 00:13:02,247
[HOLMES]
This is a rather urgent matter.

208
00:13:02,288 --> 00:13:05,500
Please help, it shan't take very
long.

209
00:13:06,668 --> 00:13:11,381
[door clicks, opens]

210
00:13:16,135 --> 00:13:19,389
[ADLER] I thought it might be
someone playing a prank on me.

211
00:13:19,431 --> 00:13:23,768
I can scarcely believe the real
Sherlock Holmes is in my house!

212
00:13:23,809 --> 00:13:27,606
I am a great fan of yours,
I'll have you know.

213
00:13:27,647 --> 00:13:31,484
My name is Miss Irene Adler.

214
00:13:31,526 --> 00:13:33,361
[HOLMES] She's not at
all like the profile

215
00:13:33,403 --> 00:13:35,530
I'd envisioned of her.

216
00:13:35,572 --> 00:13:39,951
I never expected her to be such
a light-hearted, cheeky woman.

217
00:13:49,001 --> 00:13:52,004
[ADLER] You haven't found
anything, have you?

218
00:13:52,046 --> 00:13:55,592
Are you certain that miscreant
hid it somewhere in this house?

219
00:13:55,634 --> 00:13:58,261
I am. No question.

220
00:13:58,303 --> 00:14:00,430
[HOLMES]
Go, John!

221
00:14:00,472 --> 00:14:05,435
[HOLMES] Any moment now,
I assure you, I will locate it.

222
00:14:05,477 --> 00:14:08,521
You give me your word
you'll find it?

223
00:14:08,563 --> 00:14:10,774
And you're sure
you're up to the task?

224
00:14:10,815 --> 00:14:12,484
Huh?

225
00:14:16,321 --> 00:14:18,490
You'll never
find it now, Holmes!

226
00:14:18,531 --> 00:14:20,908
I'll burn it all!

227
00:14:20,950 --> 00:14:23,578
[HOLMES wheezes]

228
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:25,037
[HOLMES]
Confound it!

229
00:14:25,079 --> 00:14:28,040
[HOLMES groans]
She's gone! But where?

230
00:14:28,082 --> 00:14:29,626
[gasps]

231
00:14:32,754 --> 00:14:35,340
Might this be what
you're looking for,

232
00:14:35,381 --> 00:14:37,550
Master Detective?

233
00:14:37,592 --> 00:14:38,968
[HOLMES]
The photo.

234
00:14:39,010 --> 00:14:41,680
Adler knew from the
very beginning exactly

235
00:14:41,721 --> 00:14:44,683
why we came here tonight!

236
00:14:44,724 --> 00:14:48,478
[WATSON] Sherlock!
Have you found the photograph?

237
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:51,439
[gasps] By Jove, the plan
actually worked!

238
00:14:51,481 --> 00:14:53,650
[HOLMES]
Uh... John...

239
00:14:53,692 --> 00:14:58,279
Your smoke bomb ruse
was perfect! A masterpiece!

240
00:14:59,614 --> 00:15:03,242
Yeah, but behind you!
There's a real fire!

241
00:15:03,284 --> 00:15:05,495
Huh? [yelps] But how?!

242
00:15:05,537 --> 00:15:07,330
It was just a smoke bomb!

243
00:15:07,372 --> 00:15:09,582
I thought you said
this worked before!

244
00:15:09,624 --> 00:15:13,753
[HOLMES] It did! But the woman
seemingly pulled one over on us!

245
00:15:13,795 --> 00:15:16,631
[ADLER]
What a terrible turn of events!

246
00:15:16,673 --> 00:15:18,299
Now the photo you
were searching for

247
00:15:18,341 --> 00:15:21,845
may have been lost in
this blazing inferno.

248
00:15:21,886 --> 00:15:23,929
And either way,

249
00:15:23,971 --> 00:15:29,602
the World's Greatest Detective
will have to answer for this.

250
00:15:35,441 --> 00:15:37,193
Now see here, Sherlock!

251
00:15:37,235 --> 00:15:40,822
What in blazes have
you done this time?!

252
00:15:40,864 --> 00:15:41,989
Uh! Oh.

253
00:15:42,031 --> 00:15:43,533
Who are you?

254
00:15:43,575 --> 00:15:47,746
And you must be,
Sherlock's landlady!

255
00:15:47,787 --> 00:15:50,456
As of today, he and
I'll be living together.

256
00:15:50,498 --> 00:15:52,208
Won't we, Sherly?

257
00:15:52,250 --> 00:15:53,959
[gasps]

258
00:15:54,001 --> 00:15:55,961
[HOLMES grumbles]
Get off!

259
00:15:56,003 --> 00:15:58,590
Why are you suddenly
behaving this way?

260
00:15:58,631 --> 00:16:00,550
It's not like that!

261
00:16:00,592 --> 00:16:02,968
It's actually quite a long
story, Miss Hudson.

262
00:16:03,010 --> 00:16:04,512
[ADLER]
Yes.

263
00:16:04,554 --> 00:16:05,889
He's right.

264
00:16:05,930 --> 00:16:08,558
But to summarize, these two
gentlemen are indebted to me.

265
00:16:08,600 --> 00:16:10,268
I do hope you understand.

266
00:16:10,309 --> 00:16:13,104
What was it? Misses Hudson?

267
00:16:13,145 --> 00:16:16,148
Oh, no. It's "Miss"
and you, Misses?

268
00:16:16,190 --> 00:16:17,817
Adler.

269
00:16:17,859 --> 00:16:20,069
Miss Adler.

270
00:16:21,780 --> 00:16:23,531
Right, lovely.

271
00:16:23,573 --> 00:16:26,075
Might I ask a favor
of you, Miss Hudson?

272
00:16:26,117 --> 00:16:27,285
What favor?

273
00:16:27,326 --> 00:16:28,912
Nothing much.

274
00:16:28,953 --> 00:16:30,580
Do you have some
clothes she might borrow?

275
00:16:30,622 --> 00:16:33,416
My clothes?! Why would I?!

276
00:16:33,458 --> 00:16:34,876
I look a mess.

277
00:16:34,918 --> 00:16:38,170
Like an ash-covered Cinderella
ever since my house burned down.

278
00:16:38,212 --> 00:16:40,089
Misses Hudson,
I hope you won't be

279
00:16:40,131 --> 00:16:42,508
like the cruel step-sisters
in the fairy tale.

280
00:16:42,550 --> 00:16:44,552
[HUDSON growls]

281
00:16:54,103 --> 00:16:57,064
Miss Adler has made
contact with Sherlock Holmes.

282
00:16:57,106 --> 00:17:00,860
Precisely as you
predicted, dear William.

283
00:17:00,902 --> 00:17:04,781
Indeed. Holmes likely
hasn't the faintest notion

284
00:17:04,823 --> 00:17:06,532
that Adler's very
objective in this

285
00:17:06,574 --> 00:17:08,993
is to worm her
way in close to him,

286
00:17:09,034 --> 00:17:14,290
the world's greatest detective,
known throughout all of London.

287
00:17:14,332 --> 00:17:16,375
[LOUIS] The more she
stays in the public eye,

288
00:17:16,417 --> 00:17:18,336
the greater the chance is
that if she were abducted,

289
00:17:18,377 --> 00:17:21,464
or assassinated it would
become a major incident.

290
00:17:21,506 --> 00:17:24,634
Thus, the government's
options are very limited.

291
00:17:24,676 --> 00:17:26,511
Nothing heavy-handed.

292
00:17:26,552 --> 00:17:30,682
Precisely, and likely
what Adler's thinking as well.

293
00:17:30,723 --> 00:17:33,476
This move should
buy her some time.

294
00:17:33,518 --> 00:17:37,730
But will it buy enough for her
to reach the negotiation table?

295
00:17:42,527 --> 00:17:46,572
[HOLMES] What is she
hoping to gain by this?

296
00:17:46,614 --> 00:17:48,282
[HOLMES]
The way things are going,

297
00:17:48,324 --> 00:17:52,203
it will be near impossible
to retrieve the photograph.

298
00:17:52,244 --> 00:17:55,039
The most I can do at this
point is keep an eye on her

299
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:57,959
to make sure she
doesn't send it.

300
00:18:00,127 --> 00:18:03,756
[HOLMES grunts]
Allow me a little smoke break.

301
00:18:03,798 --> 00:18:05,967
[ADLER]
Fine, if you insist.

302
00:18:06,009 --> 00:18:09,303
But after this, we're heading
straight to Piccadilly Circus.

303
00:18:09,345 --> 00:18:10,930
Whatever you say.

304
00:18:10,972 --> 00:18:12,390
[KATE]
No!

305
00:18:12,431 --> 00:18:15,309
I'm definitely going to
audition for the part!

306
00:18:15,351 --> 00:18:18,938
Oh, that is rich.
Do try to remember something.

307
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:20,899
The owner of the
theatre is the viscount,

308
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:23,818
who happens to be
my father. Get it?

309
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:26,320
Commoners aren't on even
footing with aristocrats.

310
00:18:26,362 --> 00:18:29,532
Which means you'll never be
a star as long as you live.

311
00:18:29,574 --> 00:18:32,827
[GIRL 12A] Doris is right.
Best to remember your place!

312
00:18:32,869 --> 00:18:35,287
[HOLMES sighs]
Should this keep devolving,

313
00:18:35,329 --> 00:18:39,250
they're bound to come
to blows. What a bother.

314
00:18:41,044 --> 00:18:42,754
[KATE]
I don't care.

315
00:18:42,795 --> 00:18:46,340
Everyone's supposed to get
an equal chance to audition.

316
00:18:46,382 --> 00:18:48,133
You really think that?

317
00:18:48,175 --> 00:18:50,887
The reality of it is you never
had a chance to begin with,

318
00:18:50,929 --> 00:18:53,264
poor Kate. How sad.

319
00:18:54,849 --> 00:18:56,141
Because you're scared.

320
00:18:56,183 --> 00:18:57,184
[DORIS grunts]

321
00:18:57,226 --> 00:18:59,520
[KATE] You're just
worried that if I do it,

322
00:18:59,562 --> 00:19:01,647
then the lead will
go to me instead.

323
00:19:01,689 --> 00:19:03,733
Be quiet.

324
00:19:03,775 --> 00:19:06,277
That's the reason you don't
want me to audition, right?

325
00:19:06,318 --> 00:19:08,071
Shut your mouth!

326
00:19:08,112 --> 00:19:09,739
[KATE yelps]

327
00:19:09,781 --> 00:19:11,866
- -[splash]
- -[HOLMES, ADLER gasp]

328
00:19:12,575 --> 00:19:16,245
[gasps, grunts]

329
00:19:16,287 --> 00:19:19,874
[DORIS giggles] Oh, no!
It looks like she can't swim!

330
00:19:19,916 --> 00:19:24,003
The sewage canal is where you
commoners belong, anyway!

331
00:19:25,964 --> 00:19:27,882
What, seriously?

332
00:19:27,924 --> 00:19:29,467
Ugh...

333
00:19:33,178 --> 00:19:34,764
[BOTH gasp]

334
00:19:34,806 --> 00:19:36,766
Adler!

335
00:19:39,978 --> 00:19:42,354
[KATE panting]

336
00:19:42,396 --> 00:19:46,150
[ADLER] Oh, bother.
We're soaked to the skin.

337
00:19:46,191 --> 00:19:49,236
[KATE]
Uh, thank you very much.

338
00:19:49,278 --> 00:19:51,363
But your dress...

339
00:19:51,405 --> 00:19:52,949
Don't worry about it.

340
00:19:52,991 --> 00:19:56,577
Saving your life is
much more important.

341
00:19:56,619 --> 00:19:57,662
But listen.

342
00:19:57,703 --> 00:20:00,539
The theatre is
not as easy a world

343
00:20:00,581 --> 00:20:02,125
as you may like to believe.

344
00:20:02,166 --> 00:20:03,375
[KATE]
Uh?

345
00:20:03,417 --> 00:20:05,837
[ADLER] I have seen far
too many parts go to people

346
00:20:05,878 --> 00:20:08,673
solely due to their connections,
or family standing.

347
00:20:08,714 --> 00:20:11,009
Wait, I know you.

348
00:20:11,050 --> 00:20:12,969
You're the Irene Adler!

349
00:20:13,011 --> 00:20:14,846
[ADLER chuckles]

350
00:20:14,887 --> 00:20:15,805
[GROUP gasps]

351
00:20:15,847 --> 00:20:18,099
[KATE]
Miss Adler! Listen.

352
00:20:18,141 --> 00:20:21,268
I want to become a prima
donna, just like you are!

353
00:20:21,310 --> 00:20:23,896
[ADLER] In that case,
listen to me, Kate.

354
00:20:23,938 --> 00:20:25,815
And remember,

355
00:20:25,857 --> 00:20:27,566
you must prove that you
are the right choice

356
00:20:27,608 --> 00:20:29,777
by letting your own
talent shine through!

357
00:20:29,819 --> 00:20:31,696
And if your skills
are strong enough,

358
00:20:31,737 --> 00:20:34,365
then someone else's
connections won't matter,

359
00:20:34,406 --> 00:20:37,035
and you'll win the day!

360
00:20:42,707 --> 00:20:44,500
[gasps]

361
00:20:44,542 --> 00:20:46,544
[KATE]
Right!

362
00:20:48,629 --> 00:20:49,881
[HOLMES]
Before you catch cold...

363
00:20:49,922 --> 00:20:51,382
[ADLER grunts]

364
00:20:52,717 --> 00:20:54,802
[ADLER chuckles]
Oh, Sherly!

365
00:20:54,844 --> 00:20:56,929
You're all but
naked now, aren't you?

366
00:20:56,971 --> 00:21:00,391
[HOLMES]
Never mind me. Put these on!

367
00:21:07,982 --> 00:21:11,276
[HOLMES] My initial profile of
the woman has been shattered.

368
00:21:11,318 --> 00:21:12,570
She's not the sort of person

369
00:21:12,611 --> 00:21:16,532
who would blackmail the king
of Bohemia over an affair.

370
00:21:16,574 --> 00:21:19,702
After you eliminate
every other possibility,

371
00:21:19,744 --> 00:21:21,704
hen whatever remains,

372
00:21:21,746 --> 00:21:26,542
must be the truth
no matter how improbable.

373
00:21:29,754 --> 00:21:33,216
We've arrived, Irene.
It's time to wake up!

374
00:21:33,257 --> 00:21:34,633
[ADLER grunts]

375
00:21:36,343 --> 00:21:38,846
[yawns]

376
00:21:38,888 --> 00:21:40,389
[ADLER, ALBERT grunt]

377
00:21:40,431 --> 00:21:42,100
[ALBERT]
Pardon me.

378
00:21:44,643 --> 00:21:47,646
Miss... Adler.

379
00:21:49,565 --> 00:21:52,193
Don't stand around! Come inside.

380
00:21:53,903 --> 00:21:56,739
Right. [grunts]

381
00:21:56,781 --> 00:21:58,323
[ADLER gasps]

382
00:23:34,962 --> 00:23:37,215
[MYCROFT] Next time
on Moriarty The Patriot  ,

383
00:23:37,256 --> 00:23:40,676
"A Scandal in the
British Empire: Act Two."
